{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261112T043311Z-004908bc", "ts": "2026-11-12T04:37:12Z", "actor": {"worktree": ".claude/worktrees/agent-a76c0614686964c8c", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/asks-last", "build": "9e93e8bdf2b4"}, "body": {"base_commit": "9e93e8bdf2b49b74c9123fcad6de1e06bbf188d1", "base_tree": "02815e8ff12356c937a6cd197b5be870acf9b592", "head_tree": "f5fe7fc1e95c367d6a497d2da18c4b40dbb48361", "head_commit": "8e44c66820626ff79b2549d7512117dd13c6b632", "dispatch_commit": "8e44c66820626ff79b2549d7512117dd13c6b632", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/session-digest.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_advisory_canary.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_complete_turn.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_empty_tasks.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_garbage.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_no_fields.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_null_message.json", "tests/test_plugin_methodology_digest.py", "tests/test_stats.py", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/session-digest.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_advisory_canary.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_complete_turn.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_empty_tasks.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_garbage.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_no_fields.json", "tests/fixtures/host_seam_golden/stop_block_null_message.json", "tests/test_plugin_methodology_digest.py", "tests/test_stats.py", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "An asks header anywhere in the turn's body takes over the close, so a quoted RUNNING \u00b7 DO NOT CLEAR example can defer the reflection and Critic gates", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. One reader, `standing_block._asks_start`/`_one_line_close`, does this. Why it broke: the reader looks for the header on any non-blank line of the whole turn, including lines inside code fences. It never checks that the header is part of the turn's tail. Once it finds one header, it reads the line just before it as the close and ignores everything after it, including the turn's real last-line close. Trace, from the code alone: a turn quotes the new shape in a fenced example (```\\n`RUNNING` \u00b7 `DO NOT CLEAR` \u2014 x\\n\\n**Needs you:**\\n1. y\\n```), then ends on its own `COMPLETE` \u00b7 `SAFE TO CLEAR` close. `_asks_start` returns the fenced header's index. `_close_line` reads the fenced RUNNING \u00b7 DO NOT CLEAR line. `turn_declares_in_flight` returns (True, 'DO NOT CLEAR'), so the reflection and Critic gates are deferred. Before this branch only the last line was read, so that turn deferred nothing. This is the reading that relaxes the gates, and the module docstring itself says it must stay narrow. A plain or decorated body line that starts with `Needs you:` has the same effect, and the guide calls the header 'exactly `**Needs you:**`', so an agent has no reason to avoid one. Fix: when looking for the header and the close before it, skip lines inside ``` fences. Add a test: a fenced RUNNING \u00b7 DO NOT CLEAR + header example followed by a COMPLETE \u00b7 SAFE TO CLEAR close must give (False, None) from `gates.turn_declares_in_flight`. Pair it with a control that has no fence. If you want a stricter guard, also give no verdict when a one-line close inside the block disagrees with the one before the header in the relaxing direction.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The Stop-block 'repeat the asks' exception is attributed to the owner, but no words of that confirmation are recorded", "recommendation": "Instance. Commit 8e44c668 changes STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER and session-hygiene's 'Answering a block' sentence so that 'Do not repeat your answer' gains an exception: re-emit the **Needs you:** block. The change record (.prawduct/changes/asks-last.md, 'which the owner confirmed') and the test comment in tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py ('owner-confirmed 2026-11-11, asks-last') both say the owner approved this. But .prawduct/owner-rulings.md has only one entry for this work, and it covers the ordering ('any actions required of the user are the last thing shown'). It says nothing about the exception, and no handoff or reflection file in the worktree records it either. building.md 'When the Owner Rules' requires the owner's words, dated, in owner-rulings.md, and the template says inferences 'stay marked as inferred where you made them'. Consequence: the next session or reviewer that questions why a blocked turn repeats part of its answer finds an owner attribution that nothing backs, and cannot tell a ruling from an agent inference. Learnings: 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority' and 'A ruling recorded only where the deciders read it is NOT recorded'. To fix, choose one: (a) if the owner did confirm it, add their words as a dated owner-rulings.md entry that points at STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER and the session-hygiene sentence; or (b) if the exception was derived from the 2026-11-11 ordering ruling, change 'which the owner confirmed' and 'owner-confirmed' to say it was inferred from that ruling, and put it to the owner at the PR.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "'Exactly `**Needs you:**`' in the guide and 'only the exact header' in the test docstring both overstate how narrow the reader is", "recommendation": "The reader opens the block on undecorated `Needs you:`, on backticked `Needs you:`, and on `**Needs you:**` with the first ask on the same line. The `plain-header`, `backticked-header` and `ask-on-header-line` cases in `TestTheAsksBlock` pin all three. Yet session-hygiene.md's Stop-hook paragraph says 'a line opening with exactly `**Needs you:**`', and the `TestTheAsksBlock` docstring says 'only the exact header opens the block'. Make both relational ('a line that leads with `Needs you:`, after markdown decoration'), as the change fragment already words it. No other edit is needed.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Two additions beyond the recorded ruling are witnessed only by the change itself: the exception to the Stop-block answer and the close-pointer check", "recommendation": "The core learnings rule that begins 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority' applies here. owner-rulings.md records the placement ruling: asks come last under a header, and the Stop hook reads past them. The fragment's 'The one exception, which the owner confirmed' (repeat the asks after a Stop block) and the test comment 'owner-confirmed 2026-11-11' are in the same commits as the change they authorize. The new `close-pointer` Stop check is a default-on recorder that runs in every governed session, and no ruling mentions it. Both follow from the ruling's intent. Either add a line under the 2026-11-11 ruling quoting the confirmation, or list both in the PR description for the owner to confirm or correct.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: no recorded dispositions to check", "recommendation": "No live plan claims `feature/asks-last` (manifest scope unbound), so there is no `governed_by:` disposition to verify, and record-lint reports governed-by-gap 0. The owner ruling in owner-rulings.md is the governing record. Read against it, the diff matches: the close comes first, the asks follow under a fixed header, and the Stop reader learns the trailing block. For future feature branches of this size, a plan seeded with `prawduct-hook jurisdiction` would give this pass something to reconcile.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The test for the answer line was loosened from 'Do not repeat your answer.' to a prefix, with a new assertion added beside it", "recommendation": "The loosening is deliberate: the sentence now continues with ', except'. `assert_the_asks_are_restated` adds an assertion on the exception's header and its 'again, unchanged', so nothing is weakened overall. No edit wanted.", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The close's form (one-line, asks block, three-paragraph) is decided again in each of three readers", "recommendation": "verdict_line, disposition and clear_verdict each run _one_line_close and then repeat the guard `if _has_asks(text): return <none>` before falling through to closing_block. _has_asks splits the lines again and calls _asks_start again, the same work _one_line_close just did. That guard is what stops a verdict from being read inside the asks block, and the RUNNING deferral relies on it, yet it lives in three copies. A future reader that goes to the public closing_block directly would skip it. The scope is a class: every reader in standing_block that works out the close's form for itself. The fix is one private _close(text) that returns a tagged reading once (a one-line tuple, an asks block that cannot be placed, or the closing block), with the three readers branching on that. This is localized and each guard has its own test, so no edit is owed now.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Two regexes in one module detect a positional above/below, and every clear-reason above-below firing also fires close-pointer", "recommendation": "_SELF_CITATIONS['above-below'] and _POSITIONAL_POINTERS both detect a positional \"above\"/\"below\", with different tails. The close-pointer tail accepts more endings, so any SAFE TO CLEAR reason that fires above-below also fires close-pointer, and the turn gets two records. governance-telemetry.md documents the overlap, so nothing is wrong today. The risk is that the next false-positive fix tunes one tail and not the other. If the overlap is meant to stay, build the above-below form from the shared positional tail so there is one definition. Otherwise, retire the above-below kind now that close-pointer owns pointers by position.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "close-pointer computes and records observations that no stats rule reads", "recommendation": "cmd_stop calls _clear_observations for close-pointer, which runs a session_work_span git probe. stats has no catch-or-slip rule for this check, so it counts firings by kind only and never reads `observed`. The telemetry doc's stated pattern is to record what was observed so a revised definition can re-read old firings, and the check fires rarely, so this is defensible. If no rule is planned, passing observed=None would skip the probe on the Stop path. No edit is wanted otherwise.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "A modified test's docstring still says the asks go above the line", "recommendation": "The docstring of test_closing_line_is_on_every_surface_that_claims_it opens \"One line \u2014 disposition, clear verdict, reason \u2014 the asks above it\". This change edited the test's body to pin the opposite placement. Nothing reads the docstring, so this is a single instance. Delete \"the asks above it\" or make it \"the asks after it, under the header\".", "files": ["tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Instruction Clarity: the guide's 'Answering a block' rule sits at the end of the longest paragraph in session-hygiene.md", "recommendation": "Framework Check 9, Cumulative Health. The Stop-hook paragraph now covers three record checks, the placement of the asks block, and the rule for answering a block. The rule an agent acts on is the last clause of that paragraph. The hook's STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER line delivers the same instruction at the moment it matters, and a test pins the two to each other, so the risk is low. If this paragraph grows again, give 'Answering a block' its own paragraph.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The new close-pointer Stop check is a builder decision that no ruling or plan covers", "recommendation": "No plan claims feature/asks-last, and the owner's ruling asked for an ordering change, not for new telemetry. The new record-only check close-pointer, its 'under any disposition' scope, and the choice to give it no catch/slip rule in stats are all the builder's. The change record states the reasons, so the decision was deliberate. It traces to the ruling's 'so nothing points above' and is consumed by stats' observed-only rows. List it for the owner to confirm or correct in the PR description, as was done for earlier unplanned decisions.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Closes the new asks-block reader cannot read leave no record, so misreads of the new shape cannot be measured", "recommendation": "The asks block adds new ways for the Stop hook to read 'no verdict' without saying so. One is a close written after the asks, which is the old close-last habit; another is two header lines, or a body line that leads with 'Needs you:'. Each fails safe: the session-end gates block, the same stance prior disposition R-1 accepted for a soft-wrapped close. So this is not a failure that goes undetected. However, nothing records that a turn had an asks block but no readable close before it. stats can therefore count pointer use (close-pointer) but not how often the new shape itself is misread. If that rate matters for judging the ruling's outcome, a record-only observation like close-pointer would measure it. No edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Shipped telemetry doc cites the check's origin by work-cycle name, '(asks-last)'", "recommendation": "In governance-telemetry.md, the sibling checks cite their origin by issue number: clear-verdict (#1044) and clear-reason (#1131). close-pointer cites the branch or scope name 'asks-last', which a consumer of the shipped plugin cannot look up once the branch merges. Delete the parenthetical, or cite an issue if one exists. This is prose that nothing reads.", "files": ["plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: the new owner-rulings entry is appended at the bottom of a file whose header says 'Newest first'", "recommendation": "Records Pass, oracle set covered: .prawduct/changes/asks-last.md and .prawduct/owner-rulings.md. The change record's checkable claims hold against the diff: the reader shape and its guards in standing_block; the deferral and clear checks unchanged apart from gates.py's docstring; the six goldens each differing by one line; the telemetry row pinned to POSITIONAL_POINTER_KINDS; and a consumer note that matches STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER and the stats row. Its one unbacked claim, 'which the owner confirmed', is the WARNING above. The owner-rulings entry is placed last, but the file's header says 'Newest first'. Entries from line 155 on are already out of order, and the file says nothing parses it. Neither file clears a Records Pass bar on any other ground. No edit is wanted beyond what the WARNING asks.", "files": [".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", ".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md"], "oid": "O-13"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `feature/asks-last`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "9e93e8bdf2b49b74c9123fcad6de1e06bbf188d1", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/asks-last.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/session-digest.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learni